\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Calculation of matrix coefficients for LUKE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Y.
 Peysson (CEA/IRFM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Grid definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The phase-space flux is discretized as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{S}\left(t_{k},\psi_{l},p_{i},\xi_{j}\right)\equiv S_{l,i,j}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 with the following non-uniform monotonic grids 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{lll}
t_{k}=t_{0},\cdots,t_{n_{t}} & t_{0}=0\\
\psi_{l}=\psi_{0},\cdots,\psi_{n_{\psi}} & \psi_{0}=0 & \psi_{n_{\psi}}=\psi_{a}\\
p_{i}=p_{0},\cdots,p_{n_{p}} & p_{0}=0 & p_{n_{p}}=p_{\max}\\
\xi_{j}=\xi_{0},\cdots,\xi_{n_{\xi}} & \xi_{0}=-1 & \xi_{n_{\xi}}=1\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which delimit elementary phase-space cells.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is discretized at the center of these cells, as shown in Fig.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "griddefmom"

\end_inset

 for the momentum space.
 In order to simplify expressions, the following notation rules are used
 for each grid and index
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
x_{m+3/2}=x_{m+++}\\
x_{m+1}=x_{m++}\\
x_{m+1/2}=x_{m+}\\
x_{m-1/2}=x_{m-}\\
x_{m-1}=x_{m--}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 A half-grid characterizes the center of each cell
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\psi_{l+}=\dfrac{\psi_{l++}+\psi_{l}}{2} & l=0,\cdots,n_{\psi}-1\\
p_{i+}=\dfrac{p_{i++}+p_{i}}{2} & i=0,\cdots,n_{p}-1\\
\xi_{j+}=\dfrac{\xi_{j++}+\xi_{j}}{2} & j=0,\cdots,n_{\xi}-1\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that the distribution function is discretized as 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t_{k},\psi_{+},p_{i+},\xi_{j+})\equiv f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

.
 The cells are also characterized by their width in each direction
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\Delta\psi_{l+}=\psi_{l++}-\psi_{l} & l=0,\cdots,n_{\psi}-1\\
\Delta p_{i+}=p_{i++}-p_{i} & i=0,\cdots,n_{p}-1\\
\Delta\xi_{j+}=\xi_{j++}-\xi_{j} & j=0,\cdots,n_{\xi}-1\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Finally, the grid intervals between two consecutive cell centers are also
 defined
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\Delta\psi_{l}=\psi_{l+}-\psi_{l-} & l=1,\cdots,n_{\psi}-1\\
\Delta p_{i}=p_{i+}-p_{i-} & i=1,\cdots,n_{p}-1\\
\Delta\xi_{j}=\xi_{j+}-\xi_{j-} & j=1,\cdots,n_{\xi}-1\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\Delta\psi_{l++}=\psi_{l+++}-\psi_{l+} & l=0,\cdots,n_{\psi}-2\\
\Delta p_{i++}=p_{i+++}-p_{i+} & i=0,\cdots,n_{p}-2\\
\Delta\xi_{j++}=\xi_{j+++}-\xi_{j+} & j=0,\cdots,n_{\xi}-2\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By convention the intervals are extended to the volume boundary points as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\Delta\psi_{0}=2\left(\psi_{0+}-\psi_{0}\right) & \Delta\psi_{n_{\psi}}=2\left(\psi_{n_{\psi}}-\psi_{n_{\psi}-}\right)\\
\Delta p_{0}=2\left(p_{0+}-p_{0}\right) & \Delta p_{n_{p}}=2\left(p_{n_{p}}-p_{n_{p}-}\right)\\
\Delta\xi_{0}=2\left(\xi_{0+}-\xi_{0}\right) & \Delta\xi_{n_{\xi}}=2\left(\xi_{n_{\xi}}-\xi_{n_{\xi}-}\right)\end{array}\right.\label{conventions}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The symmetrization of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in the trapped region is enforced implicitly by considering only one half
 of the trapped region.
 This method requires a specific treatment of the fluxes at the trapped/passing
 boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}=\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the trapped/passing boundary positions corresponding to each
 flux surface 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{Tl+}=\xi_{0T}\left(\psi_{l+}\right)$
\end_inset

 must correspond to a pitch-angle flux point 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 This effectively links the pitch-angle and radial grids and requires at
 least one of those grids to be non-uniform.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition, the symmetrization of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in the trapped region and the internal boundary conditions on 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 require to use a symmetric 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j}$
\end_inset

 grid, meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\xi}$
\end_inset

 is an even number and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\xi_{n_{\xi}/2-j}=-\xi_{n_{\xi}/2+j}\quad\text{for }j=0,\cdots,n_{\xi}/2\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{n_{\xi}/2}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The possibility to use of non-uniform grids may also be useful through the
 application of fine grids in particular region of phase space.
 This may concern regions of steep radial gradients or strong wave absorption
 for the radial grid, and the vicinity of internal boundaries 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}=\pm1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}=\pm\xi_{0T}\ $
\end_inset

in momentum space.
 The numerical accuracy may then be improved while limiting the increase
 in computational costs.
 The time grid 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}=k\Delta t$
\end_inset

 is generally chosen to be uniform.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Discretization procedure 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "discretization"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to avoid numerical singularities at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

, all terms of the Fokker-Planck equation are multiplied by the partial
 Jacobian 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{p}=p^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 This equation is discretized on the half-grid according to an implicit
 time scheme 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{gather}
p_{i+}^{2}\frac{f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k++)}-f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta t}+\left[p^{2}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{S}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k++)}\\
=p_{i+}^{2}\left[C\left(f_{M},f\right)\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}+s_{+,l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}-s_{-,l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\label{discreteFP}\end{gather}

\end_inset

 The first term on the right hand side represents the first order correction
 of the collision operator for momentum conservation.
 All right hand side terms are treated explicitly regarding the time evolution.
 If these terms represent only a small correction to the evolution of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 - this is usually the case for 
\begin_inset Formula $C\left(f_{M},f\right)$
\end_inset

 -, their explicit treatment does not restrict the use of large 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 value allowed by the implicit treatment of the flux terms for a fast and
 stable convergence to steady-state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Differential term
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The phase-space divergence term is discretized according to (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "divergencep"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left[p^{2}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{S}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}= & \left[\frac{\partial\left(p^{2}S_{p}\right)}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}-\frac{p_{i+}}{\lambda^{l+,j+}}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}\left(\sqrt{1-\xi^{2}}\lambda S_{\xi}\right)\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\label{phasediv}\\
 & +\frac{p_{i+}^{2}}{\lambda^{l+,j+}}\frac{B_{l+}}{\tilde{q}_{l+}}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\left(\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert \frac{\tilde{q}\lambda}{B}S_{\psi}\right)\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{l+,j+}\equiv\lambda(\psi_{l+},\xi_{j+})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{l}\equiv$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(\psi_{l}\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{q}_{l}\equiv\tilde{q}\left(\psi_{l}\right)$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
 & \left[\dfrac{\partial\left(p^{2}S_{p}\right)}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{p_{i++}^{2}S_{p,l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}-p_{i}^{2}S_{p,l+,i,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i+}}\nonumber \\
 & \left[\dfrac{\partial}{\partial\xi}\left(\sqrt{1-\xi^{2}}\lambda S_{\xi}\right)\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j++}^{2}}\lambda^{l+,j++}S_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}-\sqrt{1-\xi_{j}^{2}}\lambda^{l+,j}S_{\xi,l+,i+,j}}{\Delta\xi_{j+}}\label{phasediv2}\\
 & \left[\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\left(\left\Vert \nabla\psi\right\Vert \frac{\tilde{q}\lambda}{B}S_{\psi}^{\left(0\right)}\right)\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\frac{\alpha_{l++,j+}S_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}-\alpha_{l,j+}S_{\psi,l,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l+}}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 with the definitions
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\alpha_{l,j+}\equiv\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l}\frac{\tilde{q}_{l}\lambda^{l,j+}}{B_{l}}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l}=\Upsilon_{l}B_{Pl}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{l}\equiv$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon\left(\psi_{l}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{Pl}\equiv B_{P}\left(\psi_{l}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then, the phase-space flux 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{p}}$
\end_inset

 can be discretized as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{p,l+,i,j+}^{(k)}= & F_{p,l+,i,j+}f_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}-D_{pp,l+,i,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}\label{eq:fluxdis}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j+}^{2}}}{p_{i}}D_{p\xi,l+,i,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}-\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l+}D_{p\psi,l+,i,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{\xi,l+,i+,j}= & F_{\xi,l+,i+,j}f_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}-D_{\xi p,l+,i+,j}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j}^{2}}}{p_{i+}}D_{\xi\xi,l+,i+,j}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}-\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l+}D_{\xi\psi,l+,i+,j}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{\psi,l,i+,j+}^{(k)}= & F_{\psi,l,i+,j+}f_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}-D_{\psi p,l,i+,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j+}^{2}}}{p_{i+}}D_{\psi\xi,l,i+,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}-D_{\psi\psi,l,i+,j+}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l}\left[\frac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{p,l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}= & F_{p,l+,i++,j+}f_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}-D_{pp,l+,i++,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}\label{eq:fluxdis_plus}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j+}^{2}}}{p_{i++}}D_{p\xi,l+,i++,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}-\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l+}D_{p\psi,l+,i++,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}= & F_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}f_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}-D_{\xi p,l+,i+,j++}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j++}^{2}}}{p_{i+}}D_{\xi\xi,l+,i+,j++}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}-\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l+}D_{\xi\psi,l+,i+,j++}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
S_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}= & F_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}f_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}-D_{\psi p,l++,i+,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}\\
 & +\dfrac{\sqrt{1-\xi_{j+}^{2}}}{p_{i+}}D_{\psi\xi,l++,i+,j+}\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}-D_{\psi\psi,l++,i+,j+}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l++}\left[\frac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}\nonumber \end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discretization of the second derivatives associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{pp}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\xi\xi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\psi\psi}$
\end_inset

 is straightforward regarding the grids definitions
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i-,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i+,j-}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l-,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l}}\end{array}\label{dfdp_imj}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+++,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i++}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+,j+++}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j++}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+++,i+,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l++}}\end{array}\label{dfdp_imj}\end{equation}

\end_inset

However, the discretization of the cross derivatives associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p\xi}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\xi p}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p\psi}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\psi p}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\xi\psi}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\psi\xi}$
\end_inset

 is more complicated and requires to choose between several different schemes.
 The method developed here is favored because it avoids instability-prone
 double interpolations and allows internal boundary conditions to be fulfilled
 implicitly.
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i,j+++}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i,j-}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j}+\Delta\xi_{j++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+++,i,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l-,i,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l}+\Delta\psi_{l++}}\nonumber \\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+++,j}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i-,j}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i}+\Delta p_{i++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+++,i+,j}^{(k)}-f_{l-,i+,j}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l}+\Delta\psi_{l++}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l,i+++,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l,i-,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i}+\Delta p_{i++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l,i+,j+++}^{(k)}-f_{l,i+,j-}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j}+\Delta\xi_{j++}}\nonumber \end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i++,j+++}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i++,j-}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j}+\Delta\xi_{j++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+++,i++,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l-,i++,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l}+\Delta\psi_{l++}}\nonumber \\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+,i+++,j++}^{(k)}-f_{l+,i-,j++}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i}+\Delta p_{i++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\psi}\right]_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l+++,i+,j++}^{(k)}-f_{l-,i+,j++}^{(k)}}{\Delta\psi_{l}+\Delta\psi_{l++}}\\
\left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial p}\right]_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l++,i+++,j+}^{(k)}-f_{l++,i-,j+}^{(k)}}{\Delta p_{i}+\Delta p_{i++}} & \;;\qquad & \left[\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial\xi}\right]_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\dfrac{f_{l++,i+,j+++}^{(k)}-f_{l++,i+,j-}^{(k)}}{\Delta\xi_{j}+\Delta\xi_{j++}}\nonumber \end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interpolation of the distribution function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "interpolation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discretization of the Fokker-Planck equation requires values of the
 distribution function on the flux grid, which is obtained by interpolation.
 The following interpolation coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+}\left[\delta_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}\right]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{p,l+,i,j+}\left[\delta_{p,l+,i++,j+}\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j}\left[\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}\right]$
\end_inset

 are thus defined such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f_{l,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+}f_{l-,i+,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l+,i,j+}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l+,i,j+}f_{l+,i-,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i+,j}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j}f_{l+,i+,j-}^{(k)}\end{array}\label{finterp}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f_{l++,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}\right)f_{l+++,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i++,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l+,i++,j+}\right)f_{l+,i+++,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l+,i++,j+}f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i+,j++}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+++}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}\end{array}\label{finterp_plus}\end{equation}

\end_inset

but also
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f_{l+,i,j+++}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l+,i,j+++}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+++}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l+,i,j}f_{l+,i-,j+++}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i+++,j}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l+,i+++,j}\right)f_{l+,i+++,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l+,i,j}f_{l+,i+++,j-}^{(k)}\\
f_{l,i+++,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l,i+++,j+}\right)f_{l+,i+++,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l,i+++,j+}f_{l-,i+++,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+++,i,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l+++,i,j+}\right)f_{l+++,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l+++,i,j+}f_{l+++,i-,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+++,i+,j}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l+++,i+,j}\right)f_{l+++,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l+++,i+,j}f_{l+++,i+,j-}^{(k)}\\
f_{l,i+,j+++}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+++}\right)f_{l+,i+,j+++}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+++}f_{l-,i+,j+++}^{(k)}\end{array}\label{finterp_plusplus}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f_{l+,i,j-}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l+,i,j-}\right)f_{l+,i+,j-}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l+,i,j}f_{l+,i-,j-}^{(k)}\\
f_{l+,i-,j}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l+,i-,j}\right)f_{l+,i-,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l+,i,j}f_{l+,i-,j-}^{(k)}\\
f_{l,i-,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l,i-,j+}\right)f_{l+,i-,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l,i-,j+}f_{l-,i-,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l-,i,j+}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{p,l-,i,j+}\right)f_{l-,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{p,l-,i,j+}f_{l-,i-,j+}^{(k)}\\
f_{l-,i+,j}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\xi,l-,i+,j}\right)f_{l-,i+,j+}^{(k)}+\delta_{\xi,l-,i+,j}f_{l-,i+,j-}^{(k)}\\
f_{l,i+,j-}^{(k)}=\left(1-\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j-}\right)f_{l+,i+,j-}^{(k)}+\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j-}f_{l-,i+,j-}^{(k)}\end{array}\label{finterp_moins}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{p,l+,i,j-}\left[\delta_{p,l+,i,j+++}\right]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\xi,l+,i-,j}\left[\delta_{\xi,l+,i+++,j}\right]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\psi,l,i-,j+}\left[\delta_{\psi,l,i+++,j+}\right]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{p,l-,i,j+}\left[\delta_{p,l+++,i,j+}\right]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\xi,l-,i+,j}\left[\delta_{\xi,l+++,i+,j}\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j-}\left[\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+++}\right]$
\end_inset

 arise from cross-derivatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For numerical stability these coefficients must lie between 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 Linear interpolations are chosen in the radial and pitch-angle directions.
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\delta_{\psi,l,i+,j+}=\dfrac{\Delta\psi_{l+}}{\Delta\psi_{l-}+\Delta\psi_{l+}}\\
\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j}=\dfrac{\Delta\xi_{j+}}{\Delta\xi_{j-}+\Delta\xi_{j+}}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\delta_{\psi,l++,i+,j+}=\dfrac{\Delta\psi_{l+++}}{\Delta\psi_{l+}+\Delta\psi_{l+++}}\\
\delta_{\xi,l+,i+,j++}=\dfrac{\Delta\xi_{j+++}}{\Delta\xi_{j+}+\Delta\xi_{j+++}}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The variations of the distribution function in these directions are generally
 smooth and these variations vanish in the case of a uniform Maxwellian
 distribution.
 In the case of uniform grid, the corresponding interpolation coefficients
 are equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $1/2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying the same scheme to the momentum grid generally leads to slow convergenc
e rates and numerical instabilities because the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 variations in the Maxwellian distribution are strongly non-linear.
 The interpolation coefficients are calculated such that the interpolation
 is exact in the case of a relativistic Maxwellian distribution 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cha70"

\end_inset

 extended to non-uniform grids, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\delta_{p,l+,i,j+}=g_{\text{CC}}\left(\Delta p_{i}\frac{F_{p,l+,i,j+}^{(0)}}{D_{pp,l+,i,j+}^{(0)}}\right)\label{deltap}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\delta_{p,l+,i++,j+}=g_{\text{CC}}\left(\Delta p_{i++}\frac{F_{p,l+,i++,j+}^{(0)}}{D_{pp,l+,i++,j+}^{(0)}}\right)\label{deltap_plus}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{CC}}$
\end_inset

 is the Chang & Cooper function 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cha70"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
g_{\text{CC}}\left(x\right)\equiv\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{e^{x}-1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The interpolation coefficient (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "deltap_plus"

\end_inset

) is the generalization of the Chang & Cooper rule 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cha70"

\end_inset

 to the case of a relativistic distribution function and a non-uniform grid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Differential operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discrete form of the differential operators in momentum space and configurat
ion space (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "phasediv"

\end_inset

) resulting from the procedure described in Sec.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "discretization"

\end_inset

 with interpolation rules from Sec.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "interpolation"

\end_inset

 is expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left[p^{2}\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}=\sum\limits _{l^{\prime}=l-1}^{l^{\prime}=l+1}\sum\limits _{i^{\prime}=i-1}^{i^{\prime}=i+1}\sum\limits _{j^{\prime}=j-1}^{j^{\prime}=j+1}M_{l+,i+,j+}^{l^{\prime}+,i^{\prime}+,j^{\prime}+}f_{l^{\prime}+,i^{\prime}+,j^{\prime}+}^{(k)}\label{diffscheme}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where matrix coefficients are
\begin_inset Formula \[
M_{l+,i+,j+}^{l+,i+,j+}=\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discretization of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[p^{2}\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 at the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j+)$
\end_inset

 involves the value of the distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 at the center of this cell and at the center of the 26 neighboring cells
 in (cubic) phase space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Boundary conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The differential scheme (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "diffscheme"

\end_inset

) requires the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 beyond internal boundaries, which are located at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi=\pm1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=0$
\end_inset

, and also beyond external boundaries, which are located at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

.
 The conventions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "conventions"

\end_inset

) define the location of points beyond these boundaries.
 According to the discretization scheme of Sec.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "discretization"

\end_inset

 and Sec.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "interpolation"

\end_inset

, boundary conditions must therefore be used to specify the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at the following locations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
f(-\psi_{0+},p,\xi) & \qquad & f(\psi_{a}+\left[\psi_{a}-\psi_{n_{\psi}-}\right],p,\xi)\\
f(\psi,-p_{0+},\xi) & \qquad & f(\psi,p_{\max}+\left[p_{\max}-p_{n_{p}-}\right],\xi)\\
f(\psi,p,-1-\left[1-\xi_{0+}\right]) & \qquad & f(\psi,p,1+\left[1-\xi_{n_{\xi}-}\right])\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Internal boundaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The symmetry of the spherical 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi,p,\xi\right)$
\end_inset

 phase space specifies the internal boundary conditions
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f(-\psi_{0+},p,\xi)=f(\psi_{0+},p,\xi)\\
f(\psi,-p_{0+},\xi)=f(\psi,p_{0+},-\xi)\\
f(\psi,p,-1-\left[1-\xi_{0+}\right])=f(\psi,p,\xi_{0+})\\
f(\psi,p,1+\left[1-\xi_{n_{\xi}-}\right])=f(\psi,p,\xi_{n_{\xi}-})\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 However, the conservative structure of the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[p^{2}\mathbf{\nabla}_{\mathbf{p}}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{p}}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[p^{2}\mathbf{\nabla}_{\psi}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{\psi}\right]_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 and our choice of discretization schemes (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "phasediv2"

\end_inset

) means that some internal boundary conditions are intrinsically satisfied.
 Indeed, the evaluation of 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{p,l+,0,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\xi,l+,i+,0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\xi,l+,i+,n_{\xi}}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\psi,0,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 is not necessary because of the vanishing factors in front of these terms,
 which are 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{1-\xi_{0}^{2}}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{1-\xi_{n_{\xi}}^{2}}=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{0,j+}=0$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 However, boundary conditions must be enforced in the discretization of
 cross-terms when evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\xi,l+,0+,j}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{p,l+,i,0+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{p,l+,i,n_{\xi}-}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

, which requires the following operations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,0+,j+}^{l+,0+,(n_{\xi}-j^{\prime}-1)+}\rightarrow M_{\mathbf{p},l+,0+,j+}^{l+,0-,j^{\prime}+} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,0+,j+}^{l+,0-,j^{\prime}+}\rightarrow0\\
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,0+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,0+}\rightarrow M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,0+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,0+}+M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,0+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,0-} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,0+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,0-}\rightarrow0\\
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,n_{\xi}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,n_{\xi}-}\rightarrow M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,n_{\xi}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,n_{\xi}-}+M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,n_{\xi}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,n_{\xi}+} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,n_{\xi}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,n_{\xi}+}\rightarrow0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Symmetrization of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in the trapped region
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The symmetrization of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in the trapped region is enforced implicitly in the Fokker-Planck equation,
 which allows the use of large time scales as compared to explicit symmetrizatio
n methods 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kil86"

\end_inset

.
 This symmetrization is performed by solving the kinetic equation only on
 one half of the trapped region: 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq\xi\leq\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The other half of the distribution is recovered using 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(\psi,p,\xi\right)=$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(\psi,p,-\xi\right)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $-\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)\leq\xi\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 This operation creates new internal boundary conditions at the trapped/passing
 boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi=\pm\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 and at 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi=0$
\end_inset

.
 We denote 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T2}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 the indices corresponding to the grid points at the trapped/passing boundary
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j_{T1}\left(\psi\right)}=-\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j_{T2}\left(\psi\right)}=\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}\left(\psi\right)<j+<n_{\xi}/2$
\end_inset

 the symmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 means
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}=f_{l+,i+,(n_{\xi}-j-1)+}^{(k)}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the relations 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
f(\psi,p,\xi_{j_{T1}+})=f(\psi,p,\xi_{j_{T2}-})\\
f(\psi,p,\xi_{n_{\xi}/2-})=f(\psi,p,\xi_{n_{\xi}/2+})\end{array}\label{BCtrap}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 are used to evaluate the discretized fluxes at the internal boundaries.
 This operation creates a new set of coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j+}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{\prime}=i-1,i,i+1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{\prime}=j-1,j,j+1$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The discretization of 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{2}\mathbf{\nabla}_{\mathbf{p}}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{p}}$
\end_inset

 at the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T1}-)$
\end_inset

 requires the value of the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at the points 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}+)$
\end_inset

 which is removed from the calculation.
 Using the conditions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "BCtrap"

\end_inset

), a relation is created between the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T1}-)$
\end_inset

 and the points 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T2}-)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T1}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T2}-}\rightarrow M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T1}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}+} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T1}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}+}\rightarrow0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conversely, the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T2}-)$
\end_inset

 is related to both 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}-)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T2}+)$
\end_inset

.
 Because the corresponding particle passes half the time in one direction
 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T2}-)$
\end_inset

 and half the time in the other direction 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}+)$
\end_inset

, the evolution rates must be shared between both co-passing and counter-passing
 regions
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T2}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T1}-}\rightarrow\dfrac{M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T2}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T2}+}}{2} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T2}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T2}+}\rightarrow\dfrac{M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{T2}-}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{T2}+}}{2}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{n_{\xi}/2}=0$
\end_inset

 is an axis of symmetry, the following rule is applied at the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}\rightarrow M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}+M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}-} & ;\qquad M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}+}^{l+,i^{\prime}+,j_{n_{\xi}/2}-}\rightarrow0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The symmetrization of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in the trapped region requires operations on the transport rates 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}$
\end_inset

 also because the trapped/passing boundaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 are different for each flux-surface.
 The corresponding indices noted as 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}^{l+}=j_{T1}(\psi_{l+})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T2}^{l+}=j_{T2}(\psi_{l+})$
\end_inset

 satisfy the inequalities 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}^{l++}<j_{T1}^{l+}<j_{T1}^{l-}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T2}^{l-}<j_{T2}^{l+}<j_{T2}^{l++}$
\end_inset

.
 The conservation of magnetic moment - or equivalently of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}$
\end_inset

 - during radial transport implies that particle may be trapped or detrapped
 due to radial transport.
 Because different regions 
\begin_inset Formula $-\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)\leq\xi\leq0$
\end_inset

 are removed from the calculation for each flux-surface, the coefficients
 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}$
\end_inset

 must be modified accordingly :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Passing particles on the surface 
\begin_inset Formula $l+$
\end_inset

 and characterized by an index 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}^{l++}<j+<j_{T1}^{l+}$
\end_inset

 are trapped if they are transported to the surface 
\begin_inset Formula $l++$
\end_inset

.
 Because the corresponding region is removed from the calculation, the following
 operations are required on the points 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T1}^{l++}<j+<j_{T1}^{l+})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l+1)+,i+,(n_{\xi}-j-1)+}\rightarrow M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l+1)+,i+,j+} & ;\qquad M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l+1)+,i+,j+}\rightarrow0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Trapped particles on the surface 
\begin_inset Formula $l+$
\end_inset

 and characterized by an index 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T2}^{l-}<j+<j_{T2}^{l+}$
\end_inset

 are detrapped if they are transported to the surface 
\begin_inset Formula $l-$
\end_inset

.
 Because the particle passes half the time in one direction 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j+)$
\end_inset

 and half the time in the other direction 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i^{\prime}+,(n_{\xi}-j-1)+)$
\end_inset

, the evolution rates must be shared between both co-passing and counter-passing
 regions on the surface 
\begin_inset Formula $l-$
\end_inset

, and the following operations are required on the points 
\begin_inset Formula $(l+,i+,j_{T2}^{l-}<j+<j_{T2}^{l+})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l-1)+,i+,(n_{\xi}-j-1)+}\rightarrow\dfrac{M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l-1)+,i+,j+}}{2} & ;\qquad M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l-1)+,i+,j+}\rightarrow\dfrac{M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}^{(l-1)+,i+,j+}}{2}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This set of operations effectively disconnects the domain of calculation
 from the domain 
\begin_inset Formula $-\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)<\xi<0$
\end_inset

 which is to be removed from the calculation, and ensures the global flux
 conservation at the trapped passing boundary and across flux-surfaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
External boundaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Condition at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The external boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 is purely numerical as 
\begin_inset Formula $p\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 in the physical space.
 The limit 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 must be chosen such that the physical mechanisms of interest are mostly
 contained in the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq p\leq p_{\max}$
\end_inset

.
 An appropriate description of collisions, Landau damping, and ohmic heating
 requires, respectively, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\max}\gg p_{T}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\max}\gg\gamma\left(\omega/k\right)_{\max}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If the problem under interest is to calculate runaway electron losses, then
 a boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\max}\gg p_{c}$
\end_inset

 must be chosen, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{c}$
\end_inset

 is the momentum threshold above which electrons run away.
 In this case, the flux through the boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 is physical and not numerical, and corresponds to runaway losses.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With these conditions fulfilled the only physical mechanism at play near
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 is collisions.
 Using (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FDcoll"

\end_inset

) at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 shows that the diffusive part of the flux is negligible compared to the
 convective part
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left.\dfrac{\left\vert D_{pp}^{\text{C}}\right\vert /p}{\left\vert F_{p}^{\text{C}}\right\vert }\right\vert _{p=p_{\max}}=\gamma_{\max}\left(\dfrac{p_{T}}{p_{\max}}\right)^{2}\ll1\label{FDcoll2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that the Fokker-Planck equation is of quasi-hyperbolic nature in this
 region 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kar86"

\end_inset

.
 In this case no boundary condition need to be specified.
 Keeping free boundary conditions under the condition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FDcoll2"

\end_inset

) ensures that the numerical particle losses through the boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p_{\max}$
\end_inset

 are minimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Condition at 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\psi_{a}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The external boundary 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

 has a physical meaning and fluxes through the last closed flux surface
 must be accounted for.
 The general prescription is to assume a Maxwellian distribution on the
 last closed flux-surface
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
f(\psi_{a},p,\xi)=f_{M}(\psi_{a},p)\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the spatial dynamics, the external boundary at 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

 has a true physical meaning.
 The usual choice corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(t,\psi_{a},p,\xi\right)=f_{M}\left(t,\psi_{a},p,\xi\right)$
\end_inset

, which means that fast particles escape definitively the plasma when they
 cross the last closed magnetic flux surface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Particle conservation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When particles leave the domain of integration - either for numerical or
 physical reasons - the code cannot converge towards a steady-state solution.
 However, particle conservation can be restored by introducing a compensating
 source term 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{+}^{(0)}\left(t,\psi,p,\xi\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Particles are generally injected at 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

, which corresponds somewhat to cold gas injection.
 The radial profile of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{+}^{(0)}$
\end_inset

 is determined so that the particle density profile 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 is constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The integration over momentum space uses an operator derived in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "neV"

\end_inset

) and defined for any quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}\left(p,\xi\right)$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bar{\mathcal{A}}\equiv2\pi\dfrac{\tilde{q}}{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}}\int_{0}^{\infty}p^{2}dp\int_{-1}^{+1}d\xi\;\lambda\mathcal{A}\left(p,\xi\right)\label{intop}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 With this definition, the flux-surface-averaged particle density (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "neV"

\end_inset

) and the density of power absorbed (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "pabs"

\end_inset

) associated with the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}$
\end_inset

 are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle n_{e}\right\rangle _{V}=\overline{f}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle P_{\text{abs}}^{\mathcal{O}}\right\rangle _{V}=\overline{vS_{p}^{\mathcal{O}}}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 The Fokker-Planck equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FP0"

\end_inset

) is integrated over momentum space using (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "intop"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left\langle n_{e}\right\rangle _{V}+\frac{B}{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\frac{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}}{B}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert \bar{S}_{\psi}=\bar{s}_{+}-\bar{s}_{-}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The compensating source term can thus be calculated explicitly as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
s_{+,l+,0+,j+}^{(k++)}=\dfrac{3}{4\pi}\dfrac{1}{\Delta p_{0+}^{3}}\left(\bar{s}_{-,l+}^{(k)}+\frac{\left\langle n_{e}\right\rangle _{V,l+}^{(k++)}-\left\langle n_{e}\right\rangle _{V,l+}^{(k)}}{\Delta t}+\left[\frac{B}{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\frac{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}}{B}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert \bar{S}_{\psi}\right]_{l+}^{(k++)}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left[\frac{B}{\hat{q}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\frac{\hat{q}}{B}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert \bar{S}_{\psi}\right]_{l+}^{(k++)}=\frac{B_{l+}}{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}^{l+}}\dfrac{\tilde{q}_{-1,0}^{l++}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l++}\bar{S}_{\psi,l++}^{(k++)}/B_{l++}-\tilde{q}_{-1,0}^{l}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert _{l}\bar{S}_{\psi,l}^{(k++)}/B_{l}}{\Delta\psi_{l+}}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this method, particle conservation is implemented explicitly in equation
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FP0"

\end_inset

), which can sometimes lead to numerical instabilities.
 Instead, the particle loss can be compensated implicitly by normalizing
 the distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at each time step, or by imposing the Maxwellian distribution over a small
 domain around 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

 .
 Even though these two methods are not justified physically, they are usually
 preferred since they can be easily implemented in the code and provide
 a unconditionally stable alternative to ensure particle conservation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When particle losses remain a small fraction of the whole particle population,
 all three methods lead to a fast convergence and the steady-state solutions
 are nearly identical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix representation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "algorithm"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discretized 3-D Fokker-Planck equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "discreteFP"

\end_inset

) can be expressed in a matrix form
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\frac{\mathbb{P}}{\Delta t}+\mathbb{M}\right)\cdot\mathbf{F}^{(k+1)}=\frac{\mathbb{P}}{\Delta t}\cdot\mathbf{F}^{(k)}+\mathbf{I}\left(f_{M},\mathbf{F}^{(k)}\right)+\mathbf{S}_{+}^{(k)}-\mathbf{S}_{-}^{(k)}\label{Matrixeq}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}=\mathbb{M}_{\mathbf{p}}+\mathbb{M}_{\psi}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}_{\mathbf{p}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}_{\psi}$
\end_inset

 represent the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\mathbf{p},l+,i+,j+}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\psi,l+,i+,j+}$
\end_inset

 in matrix form, respectively.
 The vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset

 represents the integral part of the collision operator, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{S}_{+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{S}_{-}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 are the source and sink terms.
 The components of the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{F}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 are the values of the discretized distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 at the time step 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 organized as follows 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}^{(k)}\rightarrow\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
l=0\\
\cdots\\
l\rightarrow\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
i=0\\
\cdots\\
i\rightarrow\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
j=0\\
\cdots\\
j\\
\cdots\\
j=n_{\xi}-n_{\xi_{T}}^{l}-1\end{array}\right.\\
\cdots\\
i=n_{p}-1\end{array}\right.\\
\cdots\\
l=n_{\psi}-1\end{array}\right.\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In accordance with the implicit symmetrization scheme in the trapped region
 on the flux surface 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{l+}$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\xi_{T}}^{l}$
\end_inset

 points characterized by indices 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T1}\left(\psi\right)<j+<n_{\xi}/2$
\end_inset

 and corresponding to the domain 
\begin_inset Formula $-\xi_{0T}\left(\psi\right)<\xi<0$
\end_inset

 have been removed from the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{F}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

 and from all other vector elements in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Matrixeq"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this Victoria representation of the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{l+,i+,j+}^{(k)}$
\end_inset

, the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}$
\end_inset

 is sparse with a multiple bands structure.
 The matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix representing the momentum-space Jacobian 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 In the absence of radial transport, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}$
\end_inset

 is a block-diagonal matrix where the blocks 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 describing the momentum dynamics at location 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{l+}$
\end_inset

 are square matrices of different (decreasing) size 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{p}(n_{\xi}-n_{\xi_{T}}^{l})$
\end_inset

, each containing 
\begin_inset Formula $15$
\end_inset

 non-zero diagonals as shown in Fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fpmatrix"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the choice of spherical coordinates, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}$
\end_inset

 is a main diagonal dominant matrix if Coulomb collisions are the dominant
 physical process.
 The matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{A}\equiv\mathbb{P}/\Delta t+\mathbb{M}$
\end_inset

 is thus well conditioned even for large time step 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 allowing a stable, fast convergence to steady state.
 In some cases where other physical processes, such as strong RF quasilinear
 diffusion, dominate over collisions, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{M}$
\end_inset

 can become marginally ill-conditioned.
 In that case, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 may be used as a regularization parameter, since decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 improves the conditioning of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{A}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The introduction of radial dynamics adds several non-zero diagonals that
 connect neighboring blocks corresponding to different radial positions.
 In any case, the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{A}$
\end_inset

 is highly sparse, a property that is used to reduce the memory necessary
 to store the matrix.
 Indeed, the extended size of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{A}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\text{size}\left(\mathbb{A}\right)=\left[n_{p}\sum_{l=0}^{l=n_{\psi}-1}\left(n_{\xi}-n_{\xi_{T}}^{l}\right)\right]^{2}\lesssim n_{\psi}^{2}n_{p}^{2}n_{\xi}^{2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 For a cylindrical plasma where magnetic trapping is absent and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\xi_{T}}^{l}=0$
\end_inset

, and with a typical grid 
\begin_inset Formula $(n_{\psi}=20,n_{p}=200,n_{\xi}=200)$
\end_inset

, the total number of matrix coefficients is 
\begin_inset Formula $640$
\end_inset

 billions corresponding to a full-matrix memory requirement of 
\begin_inset Formula $5.12$
\end_inset

 Terabytes
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
On the basis of double precision where the storage of a real number requires
 
\begin_inset Formula $8$
\end_inset

 Bytes.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 However, the 
\begin_inset Formula $11$
\end_inset

 non-zero diagonals amount to less than 
\begin_inset Formula $9$
\end_inset

 million coefficients corresponding to a sparse-matrix memory requirement
 of only 
\begin_inset Formula $70.4$
\end_inset

 Megabytes
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The memory requirement of a sparse matrix is actually slightly larger as
 the index values of all non-zero coefficients must also be stored.
 Yet, the order of magnitude is not modified.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, which can be stored in the physical memory of most computers these days.
\end_layout

